  "worked_solution": "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$ we have transitivity of divisibility:\n\n$a∣b\\,$ and $\\,b∣c$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $a∣c$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let $c=10.$ Then, $10∣10$ but $4∤10.$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $5∣10.$ Therefore, we have\n$5∣10$ and $10∣c,$ which implies that $5∣c.$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, let $c=5.$ Then, $5∣5$ but $10∤5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
